{
  "id": "trapping-rain-water-stack",
  "questionId": "trapping-rain-water-stack",
  "slug": "trapping-rain-water-stack",
  "title": "Trapping Rain Water Using a Stack",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Monotonic stack",
  "patterns": [
    "monotonic stack",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "water only pools in a valley: a lower bar with a taller bar on each side. what does it mean, in terms of a stack, when the current bar is taller than the bar on top of your stack?",
    "keep a stack of indices with decreasing heights. when the current bar's height exceeds the height at the top of the stack, that top index is the bottom of a valley - pop it, since you now know both walls around it",
    "after popping the valley floor, the new stack top is the left wall and the current bar is the right wall; the trapped width is the gap between their indices, and the trapped height is min(leftWallHeight, rightWallHeight) minus the popped floor's height",
    "keep popping and accumulating trapped water for as long as the current bar is taller than the new stack top, then push the current index; repeat this for every bar in the array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using height[i] as the pocket's height directly instead of min(leftWallHeight, rightWallHeight) - the popped floor's height",
    "forgetting to stop popping once the stack top is no longer shorter than the current bar (only one pocket closes per popped floor)",
    "using strict > when comparing to the popped floor's own height once accounted for, instead of the two walls' minimum"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array `height` of non-negative integers representing the height of bars in a histogram, each bar one unit wide. After it rains, water gets trapped between the bars.\n\n**Compute the total volume of water trapped, using a monotonic decreasing stack of bar indices to find each trapped \"pocket\" as it closes.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= height.length <= 2 * 10^4",
      "0 <= height[i] <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a strictly increasing or strictly decreasing skyline (traps nothing)",
      "a single bar or an empty array (traps nothing)",
      "a deep valley bounded by two tall bars of different heights"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
